-- title:
--   §2.1, pp. 5–6, 11 — parameters, b(t), the market with unobserved drift, explicit stock (1), observation filtration F^S, θ (2), Z (3), Assumption 2.1, P̃ (4), 𝒵 (6), dual process
-- statement:
--   **The market under partial information** (§2.1–§2.2 of Chen, Jeon and Wong).
--
--   *Parameters.* A horizon $T>0$, a mandatory retirement date $0\le T_1\le T$, a constant risk-free rate $r\neq 0$ (the paper fixes no sign; $r\neq0$ is tacit in the formula for $b$), a volatility $\sigma>0$, a labour income rate $\xi\ge 0$, a discount factor $\beta>0$ and a utility cost of labour $\ell>0$. The bond is $B_t=e^{rt}$, and
--   $$b(t)=\begin{cases}-\frac{\xi}{r}\bigl(1-e^{r(t-T_1)}\bigr), & t<T_1,\\ 0, & t\in[T_1,T],\end{cases}$$
--   is minus the current value of the remaining labour income (Definition 2.5).
--
--   *Set-up* (standing assumptions of §2.1). A complete probability space $(\Omega,\tilde{\mathcal F},\mathbb P)$ with a filtration $\mathcal F=(\mathcal F_t)$ satisfying the usual conditions; a standard $\mathbb P$-Brownian motion $W$ with respect to $\mathcal F$ (continuous paths a.s., adapted, future increments independent of $\mathcal F_t$); an $\mathcal F$-progressively measurable drift $\mu$ with $\int_0^T|\mu_t|\,dt<\infty$ a.s., which the agent does not observe; and $S_0>0$. The stock is the solution of $dS_t=\mu_tS_t\,dt+\sigma S_t\,dW_t$ (1),
--   $$S_t=S_0\exp\Bigl(\int_0^t\mu_u\,du-\tfrac{\sigma^2}{2}t+\sigma W_t\Bigr).$$
--   The **observation filtration** $\mathcal F^S_t$ is generated by $(S_u)_{u\le t}$ and the $\mathbb P$-null sets.
--
--   *Densities.* The market risk premium is $\theta_t=(\mu_t-r)/\sigma$ (2) and
--   $$Z_t=\exp\Bigl(-\tfrac12\int_0^t\theta_s^2\,ds-\int_0^t\theta_s\,dW_s\Bigr)\qquad(3).$$
--   **Assumption 2.1**: $\int_0^T\theta_s^2\,ds<\infty$ a.s. and $Z$ is an $(\mathcal F,\mathbb P)$-martingale on $[0,T]$. The auxiliary measure is $d\tilde{\mathbb P}=Z_T\,d\mathbb P$ (4), and the **projected density** is
--   $$\mathcal Z_t=\mathbb E[Z_T\mid\mathcal F^S_t],\qquad t\in[0,T]\qquad(6).$$
--   The dual process started from a multiplier $y$ at time $0$ is $y_s=y\,e^{\beta s}\mathcal Z_s/B_s$ ((13) at $t=0$).
--
--   These objects are the substrate of every statement of the mission: the agent decides on the basis of $\mathcal F^S$ only, and $\mathcal Z$ is the state-price density seen through that filtration.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Processes are functions $\mathbb R_{\ge0}\times\Omega\to\mathbb R$; only times in $[0,T]$ are constrained. The stock is defined by the explicit solution of (1). The Itô integral $\int_0^\cdot\theta\,dW$ is an explicit binder $I_\theta$, constrained where it is used by `IsItoIntegral` (limits in probability of dyadic Itô sums, built on the published `EthierKurtz.itoStepSum`; it pins the integral down up to null sets, and the classical construction satisfies it). $\mathcal Z$ is used through a version `Zv` that is $\mathcal F^S$-adapted, continuous on $[0,T]$ and strictly positive along every path, and equal a.s. to $\mathbb E[Z_T\mid\mathcal F^S_t]$ at each $t\le T$ (`IsZVersion`); such a version exists because $\mathcal F^S$ is the augmented filtration of the $\tilde{\mathbb P}$-Brownian motion $\tilde W$ (Proposition 2.1), and two such versions are indistinguishable. At $t=0$ the factor $B_t/\mathcal Z_t$ of the paper's $y_s$ equals $1$ a.s. and is dropped. The measurability of $W_t$ and the joint measurability of $\mu$ are fields of the set-up, because the definition of $\mathcal F^S$ needs them; all other assumptions are separate hypotheses.
-- source:
--   Chen, Jeon, Wong, Optimal retirement under partial information, SSRN 3683553 (version of June 8, 2021), pp. 5–7 and 11, §2.1, Definition 2.1, Assumption 2.1, (1)–(6), Definition 2.5, (13)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_EthierKurtz_itoStepValue
import Definitions.Def_EthierKurtz_itoStepSum

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter
open scoped NNReal ENNReal Topology

namespace RetirePartialInfo.Duality

/-- **Parameters** (Chen, Jeon, Wong, Optimal retirement under partial information, SSRN 3683553
(version of June 8, 2021), pp. 5, 7, 9, §2.1–§2.3): the horizon `T > 0`, the mandatory retirement
date `0 ≤ T1 ≤ T` (Definition 2.4, p. 7), the risk-free rate `r` (p. 5, (i)), the constant
volatility `σ > 0` ((1), p. 5), the labour income rate `ξ ≥ 0` ((7), p. 7), the discount factor
`β > 0` and the utility cost of labour `ℓ > 0` (Definition 2.6, p. 9).

**Formalization Note.** `r ≠ 0` is tacit on p. 7: the formula for `b(t)` divides by `r`.
The paper places no sign condition on `r` and permits `T1 = 0`. -/
structure Params where
  T : ℝ
  T1 : ℝ
  r : ℝ
  σ : ℝ
  ξ : ℝ
  β : ℝ
  ℓ : ℝ
  T_pos : 0 < T
  T1_nonneg : 0 ≤ T1
  T1_le_T : T1 ≤ T
  r_ne_zero : r ≠ 0
  σ_pos : 0 < σ
  ξ_nonneg : 0 ≤ ξ
  β_pos : 0 < β
  ℓ_pos : 0 < ℓ

/-- The horizon `T` as a time index in `ℝ≥0`. -/
noncomputable def Params.Tn (p : Params) : ℝ≥0 := p.T.toNNReal

/-- The risk-free asset `B_t = e^{rt}` (SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021, p. 5, (i)). -/
noncomputable def Bt (p : Params) (t : ℝ) : ℝ := Real.exp (p.r * t)

/-- Definition 2.5, p. 7 (SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021): minus the current value of the remaining
labour income, `b(t) = −(ξ/r)(1 − e^{r(t−T1)})` for `t < T1` and `b(t) = 0` for `t ∈ [T1, T]`. -/
noncomputable def b (p : Params) (t : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  if t < p.T1 then -(p.ξ / p.r) * (1 - Real.exp (p.r * (t - p.T1))) else 0

/-- **The probability set-up and the market** (SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021, §2.1, p. 5): a
probability space `(Ω, F̃, P)`, a filtration `𝓕`, a process `W` (the `P`-Brownian motion w.r.t.
`𝓕`), the unobserved drift `µ` of the stock, and the initial stock price `S0`.

**Formalization Note.** Only the measurability that the definition of the observation filtration
`F^S` needs is a field (`W t` measurable for each `t`, `µ` jointly measurable); every assumption of
the paper on these data is in the separate predicate `Setting.Standing`. -/
structure Setting (Ω : Type*) [MeasurableSpace Ω] where
  P : Measure Ω
  𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 ‹MeasurableSpace Ω›
  W : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ
  μ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ
  S0 : ℝ
  measurable_W : ∀ t, Measurable (W t)
  measurable_μ : Measurable (Function.uncurry μ)

variable {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω]

/-- **The usual conditions** (p. 5): `𝓕` is right-continuous and `𝓕_0` contains every `P`-null
set. -/
def UsualConditions (P : Measure Ω) (𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 ‹MeasurableSpace Ω›) : Prop :=
  (∀ t : ℝ≥0, 𝓕 t = ⨅ (s : ℝ≥0) (_ : t < s), 𝓕 s) ∧
  (∀ A : Set Ω, MeasurableSet A → P A = 0 → MeasurableSet[𝓕 0] A)

/-- `W` is a standard `P`-Brownian motion with respect to `𝓕` (p. 5, (ii)): a real Brownian motion
(Mathlib's `IsBrownianReal`) with almost surely continuous paths, adapted to `𝓕`, whose future
increments `(W_u − W_t)_{u ≥ t}` are independent of `𝓕_t` for every `t`. -/
def IsFBrownian (P : Measure Ω) (𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 ‹MeasurableSpace Ω›) (W : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) :
    Prop :=
  IsBrownianReal W P ∧
  (∀ᵐ ω ∂P, Continuous (fun t => W t ω)) ∧
  (∀ t, Measurable[𝓕 t] (W t)) ∧
  ∀ t, Indep (𝓕 t)
    (MeasurableSpace.comap (fun ω (u : Set.Ici t) => W u.val ω - W t ω) inferInstance) P

/-- **Standing assumptions of §2.1** (SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021, p. 5): `P` is a probability
measure and the probability space is complete; `𝓕` satisfies the usual conditions; `W` is a
`P`-Brownian motion w.r.t. `𝓕`; the drift `µ` is `𝓕`-progressively measurable with
`∫_0^T |µ_t| dt < ∞` almost surely; and `S_0 > 0`.

**Formalization Note.** The paper asks `µ` to be "adapted to filtration 𝓕" and calls the processes
measurable; progressive measurability (the standard reading for a drift integrated in time) is
used. `S_0 > 0` is tacit (a stock price). -/
def Setting.Standing (p : Params) (st : Setting Ω) : Prop :=
  IsProbabilityMeasure st.P ∧ st.P.IsComplete ∧ UsualConditions st.P st.𝓕 ∧
  IsFBrownian st.P st.𝓕 st.W ∧ IsStronglyProgressive st.𝓕 st.μ ∧
  (∀ᵐ ω ∂st.P, IntegrableOn (fun u : ℝ => st.μ u.toNNReal ω) (Set.Icc 0 p.T)) ∧
  0 < st.S0

/-- **The stock price** `S`, the solution of (1), p. 5 (SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021),
`dS_t = µ_t S_t dt + σ S_t dW_t`:
`S_t = S_0 exp(∫_0^t µ_u du − σ²t/2 + σ W_t)`.

**Formalization Note.** With constant `σ`, (1) has this explicit pathwise solution; the stock is
defined by the formula, so no SDE solution theory is needed. -/
noncomputable def S (p : Params) (st : Setting Ω) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  st.S0 * Real.exp ((∫ u in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) t, st.μ u.toNNReal ω) - p.σ ^ 2 / 2 * t +
    p.σ * st.W t ω)

lemma measurable_driftIntegral (st : Setting Ω) (t : ℝ≥0) :
    Measurable (fun ω => ∫ u in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) t, st.μ u.toNNReal ω) := by
  have h : Measurable (fun q : Ω × ℝ => st.μ q.2.toNNReal q.1) :=
    st.measurable_μ.comp ((measurable_real_toNNReal.comp measurable_snd).prodMk measurable_fst)
  exact (h.stronglyMeasurable.integral_prod_right'
    (ν := (volume : Measure ℝ).restrict (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) t))).measurable

lemma measurable_S (p : Params) (st : Setting Ω) (t : ℝ≥0) : Measurable (S p st t) := by
  unfold S
  have h1 := measurable_driftIntegral st t
  have h2 := st.measurable_W t
  fun_prop

/-- **The observation filtration** `F^S` (SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021, p. 5): "the filtration
generated by `S_t` and augmented by `P`-null sets"; at time `t`, the σ-algebra generated by
`(S_u)_{u ≤ t}` together with the `P`-null sets. Every decision of the agent is `F^S`-adapted. -/
noncomputable def FS (p : Params) (st : Setting Ω) : Filtration ℝ≥0 ‹MeasurableSpace Ω› where
  seq t := (⨆ (u : ℝ≥0) (_ : u ≤ t), MeasurableSpace.comap (S p st u) inferInstance) ⊔
    MeasurableSpace.generateFrom {N | MeasurableSet N ∧ st.P N = 0}
  mono' := by
    intro s t hst
    exact sup_le_sup_right (biSup_mono (fun u hu => hu.trans hst)) _
  le' := by
    intro t
    refine sup_le (iSup₂_le fun u _ => (measurable_S p st u).comap_le) ?_
    exact MeasurableSpace.generateFrom_le fun N hN => hN.1

/-- Definition 2.1, (2), p. 6 (SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021): the market risk premium
`θ_t = (µ_t − r)/σ`. -/
noncomputable def θ (p : Params) (st : Setting Ω) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  (st.μ t ω - p.r) / p.σ

/-- `J = ∫_0^· φ dW` on `[0, T]` (the Itô integral against the `𝓕`-Brownian motion `W`):
`φ` is `𝓕`-progressively measurable with `∫_0^T φ_s² ds < ∞` a.s.; `J` is `𝓕`-adapted with almost
surely continuous paths on `[0, T]`; and for every `t ≤ T` there are bounded elementary predictable
integrands on the dyadic partitions of `[0, t]` (coefficient `a n k` measurable at the left
endpoint `k t / 2^n`) whose integrated squared distance to `φ` tends to `0` in probability and
whose Itô sums (`EthierKurtz.itoStepSum`) tend to `J_t` in probability.

**Formalization Note.** Mathlib has no Itô integral. This predicate characterises the Itô integral
up to a null set at each time (limits in probability are a.s. unique), and the classical
construction satisfies it. It is built on the published `EthierKurtz.itoStepSum` and
`EthierKurtz.itoStepValue`. -/
def IsItoIntegral (p : Params) (st : Setting Ω) (φ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (J : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsStronglyProgressive st.𝓕 φ ∧
  (∀ᵐ ω ∂st.P, IntegrableOn (fun s : ℝ => φ s.toNNReal ω ^ 2) (Set.Icc 0 p.T)) ∧
  (∀ t, Measurable[st.𝓕 t] (J t)) ∧
  (∀ᵐ ω ∂st.P, ContinuousOn (fun t => J t ω) (Set.Iic p.Tn)) ∧
  ∀ t : ℝ≥0, (t : ℝ) ≤ p.T → ∃ a : ℕ → ℕ → Ω → ℝ,
    (∀ n k : ℕ, k < 2 ^ n → Measurable[st.𝓕 ((k : ℝ≥0) * t / (2 : ℝ≥0) ^ n)] (a n k)) ∧
    (∀ n : ℕ, ∃ C : ℝ, ∀ k : ℕ, k < 2 ^ n → ∀ ω, |a n k ω| ≤ C) ∧
    TendstoInMeasure st.P
      (fun n ω => ∫ u in (0 : ℝ)..t.val,
        (EthierKurtz.itoStepValue t n (a n) u ω - φ u.toNNReal ω) ^ 2)
      atTop (fun _ => (0 : ℝ)) ∧
    TendstoInMeasure st.P (fun n ω => EthierKurtz.itoStepSum st.W t n (a n) ω) atTop (J t)

/-- Definition 2.1, (3), p. 6 (SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021): the exponential local martingale
`Z_t = exp(−½ ∫_0^t θ_s² ds − ∫_0^t θ_s dW_s)`, with the stochastic integral `∫_0^t θ dW` given by
the binder `Iθ` (constrained by `IsItoIntegral p st (θ p st) Iθ` wherever it is used). -/
noncomputable def Z (p : Params) (st : Setting Ω) (Iθ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  Real.exp (-(1 / 2) * (∫ s in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) t, θ p st s.toNNReal ω ^ 2) - Iθ t ω)

/-- **Assumption 2.1**, p. 6 (SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021): `∫_0^T θ_s² ds < ∞` a.s., and `Z`
is a martingale with respect to the filtration `𝓕` and the measure `P` on `[0, T]` (stated for the
process `t ↦ Z_{t ∧ T}`). -/
def Assumption21 (p : Params) (st : Setting Ω) (Iθ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ᵐ ω ∂st.P, IntegrableOn (fun s : ℝ => θ p st s.toNNReal ω ^ 2) (Set.Icc 0 p.T)) ∧
  Martingale (fun t => Z p st Iθ (min t p.Tn)) st.𝓕 st.P

/-- (4), p. 6 (SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021): the auxiliary measure `dP̃ = Z_T dP`. Under
Assumption 2.1 it is a probability measure (a fact to prove, not asserted here). -/
noncomputable def Pt (p : Params) (st : Setting Ω) (Iθ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : Measure Ω :=
  st.P.withDensity (fun ω => ENNReal.ofReal (Z p st Iθ p.Tn ω))

/-- (6), p. 6 (SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021): the projected density
`𝒵_t = E[Z_T | F^S_t]`, a conditional expectation under `P` given the observation filtration. -/
noncomputable def calZ (p : Params) (st : Setting Ω) (Iθ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (t : ℝ≥0) : Ω → ℝ :=
  st.P[fun ω => Z p st Iθ p.Tn ω | FS p st t]

/-- `Zv` is a **continuous positive version** of the projected density `𝒵` of (6), p. 6, on
`[0, T]`: for every `t ≤ T`, `Zv_t = E[Z_T | F^S_t]` `P`-almost surely; `Zv` is `F^S`-adapted;
every path of `Zv` is continuous on `[0, T]`; and `Zv > 0` everywhere.

**Formalization Note.** The paper integrates `𝒵` in time ((13), (14), (17)), which presupposes a
measurable version of the `F^S`-martingale `𝒵`. Every `F^S`-martingale has a continuous version
(`F^S` is the augmented filtration of the `P̃`-Brownian motion `W̃`, Proposition 2.1, p. 6), and
`𝒵 > 0` along almost every path (`Z_T > 0`); since `F^S_0` contains the null sets, the version may
be taken continuous and positive everywhere. Two such versions are indistinguishable, so no
statement depends on the choice. -/
def IsZVersion (p : Params) (st : Setting Ω) (Iθ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (Zv : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ t : ℝ≥0, (t : ℝ) ≤ p.T → Zv t =ᵐ[st.P] calZ p st Iθ t) ∧
  (∀ t, Measurable[FS p st t] (Zv t)) ∧
  (∀ ω, ContinuousOn (fun t => Zv t ω) (Set.Iic p.Tn)) ∧
  (∀ t ω, 0 < Zv t ω)

/-- The dual process at `t = 0` ((13), p. 11, SSRN 3683553, June 8, 2021: "where
`y_s = y e^{β(s−t)} B_t 𝒵_s/(B_s 𝒵_t)`"), started from the multiplier `y`:
`y_s = y e^{βs} 𝒵_s / B_s`.

**Formalization Note.** At `t = 0`, `B_0 = 1` and `𝒵_0 = E[Z_T] = 1` a.s. (`F^S_0` is trivial up
to null sets since `S_0` is a constant), so the factor `B_t/𝒵_t` is dropped. `𝒵` enters through
its continuous positive version `Zv` (`IsZVersion`). -/
noncomputable def ydual (p : Params) (Zv : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (y : ℝ) (s : ℝ) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  y * Real.exp (p.β * s) * Zv s.toNNReal ω / Bt p s

end RetirePartialInfo.Duality


